Context: this is one of a run of pages (previous page May 20 to Jun 9, this page Jun 13 to 29, every 4 days) computing apparent solar places from tables. The next page uses them in the 1807 orbit computation of Vesta from four places ('Berechnung der Bahn der Vesta aus vier Oertern'). For each date Gauss does the following.

(1) Table entries. Epoch values (always 68°23'55.24"; 9132.93; 834 763 837 306) plus the motion for n days (for example Jun 13: 12°48'48.29"; 355.91; 440 22 33 2). The 4-day increment is constant: 3°56'33.32" (59'8.33"/day) and 109.51 units (27.38/day). The columns are:
- mean longitude L;
- an anomaly argument in units of 1/10000 revolution, with 10000 at aphelion (my reading: the equation of centre vanishes there);
- four perturbation arguments, also in 1/1000ths. From their rates I take these as Moon (synodic month), Venus (about 580 d), Jupiter (about 400 d), and probably the lunar node for nutation (about 6800 d).
The sums are L0 = 81°12'43.73" (Jun 13), 85°9'16.85", 89°5'50.17", 93°2'23.49" and 96°58'56.81".

(2) Longitude. L0 is corrected by the following, added in sequence:
- the equation of centre: +35'44.22", +28'15.27", +20'38.64", +12'56.87", +5'11.55";
- a tiny correction on Jun 13 and 17 only (-0.45", -0.35"; meaning unsure, perhaps Mars or a table correction);
- the lunar perturbation, a sinusoid of synodic-month period: +7.41, +4.02, -2.10, -6.81, -6.86;
- Venus: -3.40, -2.77, -2.15, -1.54, -0.95;
- Jupiter: +2.38, +2.26, +1.97, +1.70, +1.45;
- a slowly varying reduction of about +36.8" (+36.83 ... +36.74). This presumably combines nutation, aberration and a table correction; I cannot be sure.
Results: λ = 81°49'10.72", 85°38'12.10", 89°27'3.31", 93°15'50.45", 97°4'38.74".

(3) Radius vector. The tabular R is corrected by lunar, Venus and Jupiter terms in units of 1e-7:
- Jun 13: (-54, -189, +121) gives 1.0159459;
- Jun 17: (-309, -187, +116) gives 1.0162361;
- Jun 21: (-352, -184, +110) gives 1.0164734;
- Jun 25: (-155, -180, +103) gives 1.0166592;
- Jun 29: (+148, -175, +97) gives 1.0167787.
Then log10 R = 0.0068706, 0.0069946, 0.0070960, 0.0071754, 0.0072264.

(4) Apparent obliquity, from the margins: ε = mean value (23°27'52.77" etc.) minus nutation (about 3.4"). This gives 23°27'49.40", 49.36", 49.24" and 49.23".

(5) Logarithms. Gauss writes log cos λ, log(R cos ε) = log R + 9.9625167, log sin λ, and log(R sin ε) = log R + 9.6000696. The same log cos ε and log sin ε are used for all dates; the 0.17" change in ε is neglected.

(6) Equatorial rectangular coordinates of the Sun (computed for Jun 13 and Jun 17 only):
- X = R cos λ;
- Y = R cos ε sin λ;
- Z = R sin ε sin λ.
Jun 13: X = +0.1445585, Y = +0.9224562, Z = +0.4004042. Jun 17: X = +0.0773157, Y = +0.9295028, Z = +0.4034629.
These are the solar coordinates needed for the geocentric-to-heliocentric reduction in orbit determination (Theoria Motus style).
